"""Reviewed commentary. Legal text is rendered separately from official source XML."""
SECTION_TEXT = '''
44-3501|Information management|Agencies,OMB|Congress gives federal information policy several goals: reduce unnecessary reporting, improve government decisions, protect privacy, and use information resources effectively. Security sits within this wider responsibility for information.
44-3502|Information management|Agencies,OMB|These definitions determine who and what the Paperwork Reduction Act covers. In particular, the definition of an agency excludes several institutions; use the applicable statute’s definition rather than assuming every federal entity has the same duties.
44-3503|Information management|OMB|The Office of Information and Regulatory Affairs sits within OMB. This section establishes its leadership and functions for administering federal information policy.
44-3504|Security,Privacy,Information management|OMB|The Paperwork Reduction Act assigns OMB government-wide information-policy oversight. Subsection (g) expressly covers privacy and security. FISMA adds a more detailed security framework in §3553.
44-3505|Information management|OMB|OMB sets government-wide information-policy goals and deadlines, including goals for reducing the public’s information-collection burden.
44-3506|Security,Privacy,Information management|Agencies,CIOs|Agency heads are accountable for managing information resources. Subsection (g) requires agencies to implement security and privacy policies and coordinate compliance with related laws; subsection (h) addresses information technology.
44-3507|Information management|Agencies,OMB|Agencies generally need OMB approval before collecting information from the public. The section sets submission, public-notice, review, and approval procedures, including special procedures for urgent collections.
44-3508|Information management|OMB|OMB decides whether a proposed collection is necessary for an agency’s work and has practical utility. It may hold a hearing before making that decision.
44-3509|Information management|Agencies,OMB|OMB may designate a central collection agency when several agencies need substantially the same information. The aim is to avoid asking the public for duplicate submissions.
44-3510|Information management,Privacy|Agencies|Agencies may share information under this section, subject to confidentiality and other legal restrictions. Sharing does not erase the protections attached to the information.
44-3511|Information management,Privacy|Agencies,OMB|Agencies maintain comprehensive data inventories and contribute to the federal data catalogue. Public access must account for disclosure restrictions and the risks identified in the section.
44-3512|Information management|Agencies|The public generally cannot be penalized for failing to respond to a covered information collection that lacks a valid OMB control number or the required notice. This is a protection for respondents, not an exemption from agency security duties.
44-3513|Information management,Oversight|OMB,Agencies|OMB reviews agency information-management activities and identifies deficiencies. Agencies must respond to OMB’s findings and recommendations.
44-3514|Information management,Oversight|OMB,Congress|OMB keeps Congress informed and reports annually on information-management activities. The reporting structure supports oversight of the Paperwork Reduction Act.
44-3515|Information management|OMB|OMB may obtain services, personnel assistance, and other support needed to carry out this subchapter.
44-3516|Information management|OMB|OMB issues the rules needed to carry out the Paperwork Reduction Act. The section also calls for specified guidance on reducing burdens on small businesses.
44-3517|Information management|OMB,Agencies|OMB consults agencies and the public when developing information policies and reviewing collections. People may also ask OMB to review whether an agency collection complies with the Act.
44-3518|Information management|Agencies,OMB|This section preserves other legal authorities and identifies activities outside the Paperwork Reduction Act’s collection-review requirements. Its exclusions must be read with the duty being applied.
44-3519|Oversight|GAO|The Comptroller General may obtain information needed to review OMB’s and agencies’ compliance with the Paperwork Reduction Act.
44-3520|Information management,Privacy|Agencies|Each agency designates a Chief Data Officer with responsibilities for data governance, access, quality, and use. The officer works with other agency officials rather than replacing their security or privacy responsibilities.
44-3520A|Information management|Agencies,OMB|The Chief Data Officer Council coordinates practices across agencies. Its membership and duties connect data management with statistical, evaluation, and information-policy work.
44-3521|Information management|Congress|This section authorizes appropriations for the Office of Information and Regulatory Affairs for the fiscal years listed. Those dates describe an authorization, not the expiration of every substantive duty in the chapter.
44-3531-to-3549|Security,History|Agencies,OMB|Congress repealed the two former information-security subchapters in 2014 and enacted §§3551–3558. The historical versions remain important for tracing duties through GISRA and the two 2002 enactments.
44-3551|Security|Agencies,OMB,NIST,DHS/CISA|FISMA’s purposes include a government-wide security framework, effective oversight, minimum controls, and recognition of the specialized treatment of national security systems.
44-3552|Security|Agencies,OMB,DHS/CISA|These definitions establish the reach of the security subchapter, including information security, federal information systems, national security systems, and binding operational directives. Definitions control the scope of the duties that follow.
44-3553|Security,Oversight|OMB,DHS/CISA|OMB oversees federal information-security policy. DHS administers specified implementation functions, including binding operational directives. Later amendments added emergency-directive and other operational authorities; the section also contains important agency and system exceptions.
44-3554|Security,Privacy,Oversight|Agencies,CIOs|Agency heads must protect information and systems in proportion to risk, assign security responsibilities, and maintain an agency-wide security program. The program includes assessments, controls, training, testing, remediation, incident handling, and continuity planning.
44-3555|Security,Oversight|Inspectors General,Agencies,GAO,OMB|Each agency receives an annual independent evaluation of its security program and practices. Inspectors General usually perform or arrange that evaluation; the statute separately addresses agencies without IGs, national security systems, reporting, and GAO’s role.
44-3556|Security|DHS/CISA,Agencies|The federal incident center assists agencies with incidents, analyzes information, and distributes alerts and assistance. Agencies’ reporting obligations connect their own response programs to this government-wide function.
44-3557|Security|Agencies|Agency heads remain responsible for protecting national security systems. The applicable standards and oversight arrangements differ from those for other federal information systems.
44-3558|Security|Agencies,OMB|FISMA preserves specified existing authorities and limits how the subchapter may be construed. These provisions help define its relationship to other laws.
44-3559|Information management|Agencies|Agencies must make their public websites mobile friendly. Congress placed this digital-service requirement in the information-security subchapter, although its subject is public website usability.
44-3561|Statistics,Privacy|Agencies|These definitions identify statistical activities, agencies, and protected information for the confidentiality and statistical-efficiency subchapter.
44-3562|Statistics,Privacy|OMB,Agencies|OMB coordinates implementation of the statistical confidentiality framework. Agencies remain responsible for the activities and information covered by it.
44-3563|Statistics,Privacy|Agencies,OMB|Statistical agencies and units have responsibilities for producing credible, accurate, relevant, and timely statistical information. The section establishes organizational protections and standards for this work.
44-3564|Statistics,Privacy|Agencies|This section explains how the statistical confidentiality subchapter interacts with other laws. Restrictions on disclosure and use must be read together with these savings provisions.
44-3571|Statistics,Privacy|Congress|Congress sets out its findings on confidentiality and public trust in statistical work. The operative protections follow in §3572.
44-3572|Statistics,Privacy,Security|Agencies|Information acquired under a pledge of confidentiality for exclusively statistical purposes receives use and disclosure protections. The section also provides penalties for prohibited disclosures.
44-3575|Statistics|Congress|Congress explains why sharing business data among designated statistical agencies can improve statistics and reduce reporting burdens.
44-3576|Statistics,Privacy|Agencies|Designated statistical agencies may share covered business data under specified safeguards. Access is limited by the purposes, agreements, and confidentiality duties in this section.
44-3581|Statistics,Privacy|Agencies,OMB|Agencies must make data assets available to statistical agencies and units under a presumption of accessibility, subject to legal limits and required protections.
44-3582|Statistics,Privacy,Security|Agencies,OMB|OMB establishes a process for expanding secure access to confidential statistical data. Access arrangements must protect the information while supporting statistical analysis.
44-3583|Statistics,Privacy|Agencies,OMB|This section establishes a standard application process for access to data for developing evidence. Agencies must evaluate requests under the applicable access and confidentiality rules.
44-3601|Information management|Agencies,OMB|The E-Government chapter defines terms used in its government-wide digital-management provisions. The FedRAMP provisions add their own definitions in §3607.
44-3602|Information management,Security,Privacy|OMB|OMB’s Office of Electronic Government coordinates digital-government policy. Its responsibilities connect IT investment, service delivery, information security, privacy, and interagency collaboration.
44-3603|Information management,Security|CIOs,OMB|The Chief Information Officers Council brings agency CIOs together to improve federal IT practices. Its statutory duties include recommendations, shared approaches, and coordination with related functions.
44-3604|Information management|OMB,GSA|The E-Government Fund supports projects that expand electronic government. This section sets project-selection, management, and reporting requirements.
44-3605|Information management|OMB,Agencies|The Administrator of Electronic Government must establish a program to encourage innovative solutions and convene a multiagency team to help assess proposals.
44-3606|Information management,Oversight|OMB,Agencies,Congress|OMB reports to Congress on implementation of the E-Government Act using information supplied by agencies.
44-3607|Cloud/FedRAMP,Security|Agencies,GSA,OMB|FedRAMP has its own statutory vocabulary. Congress incorporates A-130’s definitions of authorization to operate and federal information, and NIST SP 800-145’s definition of cloud computing.
44-3608|Cloud/FedRAMP,Security|GSA|Congress establishes FedRAMP within the General Services Administration as a government-wide approach to assessing and authorizing cloud services.
44-3609|Cloud/FedRAMP,Security|GSA|GSA administers FedRAMP, develops its processes, supports reuse, and coordinates with agencies and other participants. The section supplies the program’s principal operating responsibilities.
44-3610|Cloud/FedRAMP,Security|GSA,Agencies|The FedRAMP Board provides input and recommendations on standards, requirements, and priorities. Its statutory role differs from an agency official’s decision to authorize use of a service.
44-3611|Cloud/FedRAMP,Security|GSA|GSA may determine whether FedRAMP uses an independent assessment service to analyze, validate, and attest to security-assessment materials. A cloud-service assessment is distinct from the annual agency-wide evaluation required by §3555.
44-3612|Cloud/FedRAMP,Security|GSA|Cloud providers seeking FedRAMP authorization must disclose specified foreign interests. GSA establishes the process and determines what information is required under this section.
44-3613|Cloud/FedRAMP,Security|Agencies|Agencies must follow the statutory approach to using FedRAMP, reusing assessments, and coordinating authorization decisions. Agency responsibilities continue alongside GSA’s program administration.
44-3614|Cloud/FedRAMP,Security|OMB|OMB issues guidance for agency implementation of FedRAMP and oversees compliance. This is a direct statutory assignment to OMB.
44-3615|Cloud/FedRAMP,Oversight|GSA,GAO,Congress|GSA reports to Congress and GAO reviews FedRAMP under the schedules and subjects specified here. These program-level reports supplement other federal security oversight.
44-3616|Cloud/FedRAMP,Oversight|GSA|The Federal Secure Cloud Advisory Committee provides recommendations on FedRAMP and cloud adoption. Its composition, reporting, and termination rules are specified here.
5-401|Oversight|Inspectors General|Definitions establish which agencies and officials are covered by the Inspector General provisions in this chapter.
5-402|Oversight|Inspectors General|Congress establishes independent and objective IG units to audit and investigate agency programs, promote effectiveness, and keep agency leadership and Congress informed.
5-403|Oversight|Inspectors General|This section governs IG appointments, supervision, removal, and related protections. These arrangements support the independence of oversight work, including security evaluations.
5-404|Oversight|Inspectors General|IGs conduct and supervise audits and investigations, review legislation, recommend improvements, and report problems. These general responsibilities support the specific annual security-evaluation duty in Title 44.
5-405|Oversight|Inspectors General,Congress|IGs report their activities and significant findings to agency heads and Congress. These reporting duties operate alongside FISMA’s annual evaluation requirements.
5-406|Oversight|Inspectors General|IGs receive powers to obtain records, conduct investigations, seek assistance, and perform their duties. The section defines both their access tools and applicable limits.
5-407|Oversight|Inspectors General,Agencies|Employees may report suspected wrongdoing to an IG. The section protects confidentiality and addresses reprisals for protected reports.
5-408|Oversight,Security|Inspectors General|Special provisions govern the Defense Department IG, including the Secretary’s authority in specified sensitive matters and associated notice requirements.
5-424|Oversight|Inspectors General|The Council of the Inspectors General on Integrity and Efficiency coordinates the IG community and promotes standards and training. FISMA expressly includes the Council in developing evaluation guidance.
5-552a|Privacy,Security|Agencies|The Privacy Act governs agency records about individuals in systems of records. Its duties include limits on disclosure, access and amendment rights, notices, and safeguards against threats to security or integrity.
5-1013|Oversight|Agencies|This section governs termination and renewal of federal advisory committees. FedRAMP’s committee provision refers to the predecessor provision of the Federal Advisory Committee Act.
6-650|Security|DHS/CISA|These definitions apply to the statutory subchapter containing CISA’s authorities. They must be read with the definitions in the particular authority being used.
6-651|Security|DHS/CISA|This short definition provision supplies the meaning of the term used in its part of the Homeland Security Act.
6-652|Security|DHS/CISA|Congress establishes CISA within DHS and assigns its Director cybersecurity and infrastructure-security functions. The agency supports the Secretary’s responsibilities for federal information systems.
6-659|Security|DHS/CISA,Agencies|The national cybersecurity and communications integration center coordinates information sharing and assistance. Its responsibilities extend beyond federal agency systems and include privacy and civil-liberties safeguards.
6-660|Security|DHS/CISA|DHS develops cybersecurity plans under the circumstances and consultation requirements specified here.
6-663|Security|DHS/CISA,Agencies|DHS deploys and operates intrusion-detection and prevention capabilities for federal systems. The authority includes privacy protections and limits, and is subject to the termination provision in §1525.
6-1521|Security|DHS/CISA,Agencies|These definitions govern the Federal Cybersecurity Enhancement Act provisions, including the scope of covered agencies and systems.
6-1522|Security|DHS/CISA,Agencies|DHS develops and implements advanced internal defenses for federal systems under the consultation and operational requirements in this section.
6-1523|Security|Agencies,OMB|Covered agencies implement specified cybersecurity capabilities, including identity, access, and data-protection measures, under the section’s deadlines and exceptions.
6-1524|Security,Oversight|Inspectors General,DHS/CISA,Agencies|This section requires assessments and reports on specified cybersecurity practices. Its IG-related work and DHS reporting requirements have their own scope and timing.
6-1525|Security|DHS/CISA|The current Code snapshot ends §663 authority and §1524(c) reporting on December 11, 2026. The rule of construction preserves the specified liability protection for qualifying assistance provided while authorized.
15-278g-3|Security,Standards|NIST,Commerce|Congress directly assigns NIST the development of security standards, guidelines, and minimum requirements for covered federal systems. NIST submits standards to Commerce; the statute also assigns research, assistance, and consultation duties.
15-278g-4|Security,Privacy,Standards|NIST,Commerce|The Information Security and Privacy Advisory Board advises on standards and related issues. Its work is part of NIST’s statutory framework.
40-11101|Information management|Agencies,CIOs|These definitions establish the reach of federal IT-management law, including information technology and executive agencies.
40-11302|Information management|OMB|OMB promotes effective federal acquisition and use of IT through capital planning, investment controls, and budget oversight.
40-11303|Information management,Oversight|OMB|OMB uses performance and results to evaluate agency IT management. Its enforcement tools connect budget decisions with accountability for investments.
40-11312|Information management|Agencies|Agency heads establish capital-planning and investment-control processes for IT. These processes connect investments with budgets, performance, and management decisions.
40-11313|Information management|Agencies|Agencies measure how IT supports their missions and use results to improve performance and accountability.
40-11314|Information management,Acquisition|Agencies|Agency heads have authority to acquire and manage IT, subject to the statutory framework and oversight provisions.
40-11315|Information management,Security|CIOs,Agencies|Agency CIOs advise leadership, develop and maintain an integrated IT architecture, and promote effective information-resources management. These duties sit alongside the CIO’s FISMA security responsibilities.
40-11319|Information management,Acquisition|CIOs,Agencies,OMB|FITARA strengthened CIO involvement in budgets, investments, and portfolio management. This section includes agency-specific scope rules and exceptions.
40-11331|Security,Standards|Commerce,NIST,OMB|Commerce prescribes federal information-system standards based on NIST’s work and makes specified security standards binding. The President retains a disapproval power, and national security systems follow a separate authority path.
41-1321|Acquisition,Security|Agencies|Definitions establish the scope of the federal acquisition supply-chain security framework, including covered articles and supply-chain risk.
41-1322|Acquisition,Security|Agencies,OMB|The Federal Acquisition Security Council brings designated agencies together to coordinate supply-chain security.
41-1323|Acquisition,Security|Agencies,DHS/CISA|The Council assesses supply-chain risks and recommends action. The section sets procedures for exclusion and removal orders and identifies the officials who may issue them.
41-1324|Acquisition,Security|Agencies|The Council prepares a strategic plan for carrying out its supply-chain security responsibilities.
41-1325|Acquisition,Security,Oversight|Agencies,Congress|The Council reports annually to Congress on its activities and recommendations.
41-1326|Acquisition,Security|Agencies|Executive agencies assess supply-chain risks and comply with applicable exclusion and removal orders. FISMA expressly connects agency security responsibilities to this framework.
41-1327|Acquisition,Security|Agencies|This section establishes judicial review procedures for specified supply-chain security orders and protects sensitive information during review.
41-1328|Acquisition,Security|Agencies|The supply-chain security subchapter terminates on December 31, 2033 under the current Code text. Congress extended the earlier termination provision in 2022.
'''
