CLAUSES={
 '44-3504/g':('Privacy and security were already part of information policy','The PRA assigns OMB oversight of privacy and security policy and coordination with other information-management laws. This authority is broader than the later FISMA security-program provisions.'),
 '44-3506/g':('Agencies must implement privacy and security policy','This PRA duty requires agency implementation and accountability. FISMA supplies additional, more detailed program requirements; A-130 brings these responsibilities together in agency policy.'),
 '44-3552/b/1':('What makes a directive binding','A binding operational directive is a compulsory direction to an agency for safeguarding federal information and systems. Its scope depends on the rest of this subchapter, including the exceptions in §3553.'),
 '44-3553/a':('OMB sets policy and oversees implementation','OMB oversees policy, standards adoption, risk-based protection, DHS performance, and agency compliance. Its role includes coordinating security with other information-management policies.'),
 '44-3553/b':('DHS administers implementation','DHS develops binding operational directives, monitors agency implementation, and supplies operational assistance within this subsection’s scope. These functions work under OMB policy.'),
 '44-3553/h':('Emergency directives address substantial threats','Congress added this emergency authority in 2015. It specifies when the Secretary may direct agency action and requires coordination and notification; it is distinct from the ordinary BOD provision.'),
 '44-3554/a':('Agency heads remain accountable','Agency heads must protect agency information and systems, including covered systems operated by contractors or other organizations on the agency’s behalf. Delegating security work does not remove this responsibility.'),
 '44-3554/b':('The agency-wide security program','The statute specifies the program’s components. Risk assessments, policies, training, testing, remediation, incident response, and continuity planning form an ongoing program rather than a one-time authorization exercise.'),
 '44-3554/b/5':('Testing at least annually','The frequency must reflect risk, with a statutory floor of at least annual testing and evaluation. This agency program requirement is separate from the independent evaluation in §3555.'),
 '44-3554/c':('Reporting on the security program','Agencies report on their security programs and compliance to the recipients specified here. Other subsections and uncodified breach provisions add incident-related reporting duties.'),
 '44-3555/a':('An independent evaluation every year','The evaluation tests a representative subset of systems and assesses the effectiveness of agency policies and practices. The law calls for separate treatment of national security systems where appropriate.'),
 '44-3555/b':('Who performs the evaluation','For agencies with an IG, the IG performs the evaluation or determines the use of an independent external auditor. Agencies outside that category engage an independent external auditor through the agency head.'),
 '44-3555/b/1':('A citation changed in 2022; the duty did not begin then','The 2022 amendment replaced the reference to the Inspector General Act with Title 5, chapter 4. Annual independent security evaluations appeared in GISRA in 2000 and continued through the 2002 and 2014 legislation.'),
 '44-3555/c':('National security systems have a separate arrangement','An entity designated by the agency head performs these evaluations. The evaluation must protect vulnerability information in proportion to risk and comply with applicable law.'),
 '44-3555/d':('Existing work can support the evaluation','The evaluator may use an audit, evaluation, or report about the agency’s programs or practices. This permits reuse without removing the annual evaluation requirement.'),
 '44-3555/e':('The agency head sends the results to OMB','OMB establishes the submission date. Agency leadership transmits the results even where the IG performed the evaluation.'),
 '44-3555/f':('Protect sensitive evaluation information','Agencies and evaluators protect information whose disclosure could harm information security. The protections must reflect risk and comply with applicable laws and regulations.'),
 '44-3555/g':('OMB reports evaluation results to Congress','OMB summarizes the results in its annual report while protecting information about vulnerabilities and national security systems. Intelligence-related evaluations use the congressional channels specified here.'),
 '44-3555/h':('GAO provides another layer of oversight','The Comptroller General periodically evaluates agency security policies and implementation of FISMA and reports to Congress.'),
 '44-3555/i':('GAO may assist evaluators','GAO may provide technical assistance to IGs or agency heads, including testing controls and procedures.'),
 '44-3555/j':('OMB coordinates evaluation guidance','OMB develops evaluation guidance in consultation with DHS, the CIO Council, CIGIE, and other appropriate participants. Reporting metrics implement this assignment; they do not create the underlying annual duty.'),
 '44-3607/b/3':('Congress incorporates A-130 terminology','The FedRAMP law adopts A-130’s definitions of authorization to operate and federal information, including successor documents. Here the statutory text expressly points to the circular.'),
 '44-3607/b/4':('Congress incorporates NIST’s cloud definition','The law uses the cloud-computing definition in NIST SP 800-145 or its successor. The reference incorporates a definition; it does not make every NIST publication binding.'),
 '44-3614/1':('OMB must issue FedRAMP guidance','This is an explicit statutory direction to OMB. The 2024 FedRAMP memorandum supplies implementation guidance for the program.'),
 '15-278g-3/a':('NIST receives its assignment directly from Congress','The statute establishes NIST’s standards and guidelines responsibilities for covered federal systems. A-130 directs agencies to use the resulting materials within OMB’s management policy.'),
 '15-278g-3/b':('Categorization and minimum requirements','Congress specifies categories of information and systems, minimum requirements, incident guidance, and national-security-system guidance. FIPS 199 and FIPS 200 are key products in this standards framework.'),
 '40-11331/a':('Commerce prescribes the standards','Commerce acts on standards and guidelines developed by NIST. National security systems are addressed separately in paragraph (2).'),
 '40-11331/b':('How standards become mandatory','The statute requires specified information-security standards to be compulsory and binding. A Special Publication’s practical force depends on the applicable statute, policy, standard, or contract that calls for its use.'),
 '5-552a/e/10':('The Privacy Act also requires safeguards','The agency must establish appropriate administrative, technical, and physical safeguards for records. This privacy-specific duty predates FISMA.'),
}

